## Food and drink

- drink plenty of **water**
- cut down on **alcohol**, especially beer and spirits, and have several alcohol-free days a week
- avoid **sugary drinks** and drinks sweetened with fructose
- eat less **red meat, offal** (such as liver and kidneys), and some **seafood** such as sardines, mussels and anchovies
- low fat dairy foods may help lower urate

A strict diet is not needed, and diet alone rarely controls gout.

## Weight and exercise

Losing weight slowly, if you are overweight, can lower urate. Avoid crash diets, which can trigger flares.

## Have a flare plan

Agree with your GP what to take when a flare starts, and keep some at home.

## Look after your heart and kidneys

Gout is linked to heart disease, high blood pressure, diabetes and kidney disease. Keep up with your regular health checks.

## Support

The **UK Gout Society** has free information and diet sheets.
